WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.120
Put a mouse in a chamber and cool the air, one step at a time.

2
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.840
What happens to the heat it makes?

3
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.660
And why does nothing happen at first?

4
00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.030
The first film drew the body as a tank.

5
00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.230
Heat made flows in through the orange tap.

6
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.390
Heat leaks out through a blue outlet whose width can change.

7
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.710
Now we watch what holding the level costs.

8
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.920
In this model, we leave out sun and evaporation.

9
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.980
So heat made must match the blue leak.

10
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.260
We read heat made from the oxygen the mouse uses.

11
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.760
At each step, we plot one orange point.

12
00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:45.643
At first, the orange points stay flat.

13
00:00:46.843 --> 00:00:50.143
The mouse holds its temperature without making extra heat.

14
00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:53.343
Instead, it narrows the blue outlet.

15
00:00:54.723 --> 00:00:56.523
Skin vessels close, and fur lifts.

16
00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:00.297
This flat stretch is the thermoneutral zone.

17
00:01:01.477 --> 00:01:06.537
The resting rate along it is the basal metabolic rate, measured at rest and not

18
00:01:06.537 --> 00:01:07.537
digesting.

19
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.450
But the outlet can only get so narrow.

20
00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:16.410
At the lower critical temperature, our model mouse reaches its narrowest outlet.

21
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.550
Its conductance is at its minimum.

22
00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:23.743
Below that, the model holds the outlet at its minimum.

23
00:01:25.223 --> 00:01:26.943
Now only the orange tap can answer.

24
00:01:28.263 --> 00:01:30.863
Each degree colder adds the same extra heat.

25
00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:33.743
So the points climb in a straight line.

26
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:39.623
Extend that line toward warmer air, and it reaches zero at body temperature.

27
00:01:40.923 --> 00:01:42.543
That zero is only arithmetic.

28
00:01:43.943 --> 00:01:46.083
A living animal always makes some heat.

29
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:51.393
Per Scholander, Laurence Irving and colleagues measured curves like this.

30
00:01:52.513 --> 00:01:57.153
They plotted resting heat made against air temperature, for mammals from the arctic

31
00:01:57.153 --> 00:01:58.413
and from the tropics.

32
00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:04.657
Now give an animal thicker fur, with the same basal rate and body temperature.

33
00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:08.817
Does the bend move to colder air, or to warmer air?

34
00:02:15.283 --> 00:02:21.943
Colder. A narrower outlet can carry the basal heat away only across a bigger gap.

35
00:02:23.183 --> 00:02:25.263
And below the bend, the climb is shallower.

36
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:28.037
That is the pattern they found.

37
00:02:29.417 --> 00:02:34.177
Thick-furred arctic mammals stayed on their flat line into far colder air than

38
00:02:34.177 --> 00:02:40.157
tropical ones. The difference lay mainly in insulation, not in a higher basal rate.

39
00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:44.507
So the curve is the heat balance, plotted against the air.

40
00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:50.147
Conductance sets where the bend falls, and how steeply the cost climbs below it.
